⏺ Top 10 by real-world install rate (rough order — varies by survey):

  1. pgvector — vector embeddings (cosine/L2/IP distance, IVF/HNSW indexes).
  Exploded since 2023 for RAG/AI apps. → Basin: replaced by native vector(N) +
  HNSW (ADR 0003).
  2. PostGIS — geospatial primitives, R-tree-based GiST indexes, projection
  support. Big in mapping, logistics, climate. → Basin: not on roadmap;
  recommend dedicated geo DB or sidecar PG (ADR 0002).
  3. pg_stat_statements — query performance / hot-query tracking. Almost always
  on in production. → Basin: covered by tracing spans + OTLP export; structural
  equivalent.
  4. uuid-ossp — UUID generation (uuid_generate_v4()). Mostly superseded by
  gen_random_uuid() since PG 13. → Basin: implementable as a built-in if
  ORM-blocking; UUID column type planned.
  5. pgcrypto — bcrypt, sha2, AES, gen_random_uuid(). Used for password hashing
  and token gen. → Basin: same — case-by-case as built-in if ORM blocking.
  6. hstore — key-value column type. Largely replaced by JSONB but still in
  legacy schemas. → Basin: not planned; JSONB on roadmap.
  7. TimescaleDB — time-series hypertables, native compression, continuous
  aggregates. Popular for IoT/observability. → Basin: not on roadmap; Iceberg
  snapshot semantics partially overlap but TimescaleDB's hypertable model is a
  different shape (ADR 0002).
  8. Citus — distributed PG (sharding + reference tables). Now Microsoft-owned,
  also in Azure Cosmos for PG. → Basin: not on roadmap; sharding is structural
  in Basin via per-tenant prefix.
  9. pg_trgm — trigram-based fuzzy text matching, GIN indexes for LIKE '%foo%'.
  → Basin: not on roadmap; would be a real gap for "search-y" workloads.
  10. pg_cron — cron-like in-DB scheduler. → Basin: out of scope; expect users
  to schedule externally (Workers, GitHub Actions, etc).

  ---
  Honorable mentions that show up in 5–15% of installs:

  - postgres_fdw / dblink — foreign-table queries to remote PG. (Not planned.)
  - citext — case-insensitive text. (Trivial as a built-in if needed.)
  - pg_partman — partition management. (Iceberg covers the underlying need.)
  - pgaudit — audit logging. (Tracing spans cover most of this.)
  - plpython3u / plperl — alt procedural languages. (Not planned — see
  ADR 0002.)

  ---
  The honest gap analysis for Basin's "no extensions" posture:

  ┌────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
  │     Extension      │       Coverage       │     Realistic blockers      │
  ├────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
  │ pgvector           │ structurally         │ none — ADR 0003             │
  │                    │ replaced             │                             │
  ├────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
  │ pg_stat_statements │ structurally         │ none                        │
  │                    │ replaced (OTLP)      │                             │
  ├────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
  │ uuid-ossp /        │ trivial as built-ins │ small task, ORM-driven      │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ PostGIS            │ gap                    │ dedicated geo DB          │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ TimescaleDB        │ partial via Iceberg    │ continuous-aggregate      │
  │                    │ snapshots              │ shape mismatch            │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ Citus              │ structurally replaced  │ none — bucket prefix is   │
  │                    │                        │ the shard                 │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ pg_trgm            │ gap                    │ full-text/fuzzy search    │
  │                    │                        │ story TBD                 │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ pg_cron            │ gap                    │ external scheduler        │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ hstore             │ gap                    │ JSONB on roadmap          │
  ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
  │ pg_partman         │ structurally replaced  │ none                      │
  │                    │ (Iceberg manifests)    │                           │
  └────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘

  The blockers that would make a real customer reject Basin are pgvector,
  PostGIS, and pg_trgm. pgvector is already handled; PostGIS and pg_trgm are
  honest gaps that would push geo-centric apps and search-heavy apps elsewhere.
  Everything else is either structurally covered, trivially implementable as a
  built-in case-by-case, or out of scope on purpose.
